  "objective": "Evaluate web content to determine whether text alternatives are provided for images, video, and other non-text content.",
  "recent_history": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I need to evaluate whether non-text content has good alternatives. I know images sometimes need alt text, but I am not sure how to decide what counts as good or when something should be ignored."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Start with one rule: don\u2019t ask \u201cdoes it have text?\u201d first; ask \u201cwhat purpose is this non-text content serving here?\u201d Under WCAG 1.1.1, the alternative is good if it serves the equivalent purpose in context, and if the content is pure decoration, only visual formatting, or not meant to be understood by users, it should be implemented so assistive technology can ignore it rather than announcing it. So a useful image needs text that gives the same needed information, a control image needs a name that tells what it does, and a decorative border should be ignored instead of described.\n\nTry this case: a magnifying-glass image that opens search when clicked \u2014 would a good alternative describe how it looks, or what it does?"
    }
  ],
  "last_tutor_response": "Start with one rule: don\u2019t ask \u201cdoes it have text?\u201d first; ask \u201cwhat purpose is this non-text content serving here?\u201d Under WCAG 1.1.1, the alternative is good if it serves the equivalent purpose in context, and if the content is pure decoration, only visual formatting, or not meant to be understood by users, it should be implemented so assistive technology can ignore it rather than announcing it. So a useful image needs text that gives the same needed information, a control image needs a name that tells what it does, and a decorative border should be ignored instead of described.\n\nTry this case: a magnifying-glass image that opens search when clicked \u2014 would a good alternative describe how it looks, or what it does?",
